From e1acfdfbdf226da828e86227df581c27aeb1e0cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CyberAshven <130158492+CyberAshven@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 11:56:22 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] rpc: add scantxoutset

scantxoutset "action" ( [scanobjects,...] ) finds the unspent outputs
whose locking bytecode or token category a caller names, with one walk
of the UTXO set and no index. "start" takes addr(), raw() and tok()
scan objects, as strings or {"desc", "range"} objects; "status" gives
the progress of the running scan and "abort" stops it, one scan at a
time. The answer has the fields BCHN 29.1.0 gives: success,
searched_items, the unspents in outpoint order with tokenData as
gettxout shows it, total_amount and token_total_amounts. Pickaxe finds
a token covenant output with it on a first start (#1092).

The caller names what it looks for, so it is off unless the operator
sets rpc.scantxoutset, and off it answers -32601 naming that setting.
On, it answers callers on loopback and the single addresses named by
rpc.scantxoutset_caller, and refuses every other caller before its
parameters are read; a caller entry that names more than one host is
refused at startup, and a node with it on and bound beyond loopback
logs one warning. Scan objects and outputs found are logged at no level
and named in no error: an error names a scan object by its position.
What the server read and wrote for the request is zeroed once the
answer is written. A scan takes at most 1000 scan objects, a raw()
script of at most 10000 bytes, and an answer of at most 32 MiB.

The walk runs on a thread of its own under a read lease of the UTXO
store, not the exclusive window, so lookups, mempool admission and
templates go on beside it. Every 1024 entries it asks whether block
connection waits for the window, and if so it stops, lets the block in
and walks again: a block waits at most 1024 entries for a scan, and a
complete scan saw the set between two blocks.
