+## scantxoutset
+
+`scantxoutset "action" ( [scanobjects,...] )` finds, with one walk of the UTXO
+set and no index, the unspent outputs whose locking bytecode or token category
+a caller names. It is for clients the operator runs against their own node:
+[Pickaxe](https://github.com/CyberAshven/pickaxe-miner), for one, finds a token
+covenant output with it on a first start. The caller names what it looks for,
+so a node that answered it to anyone would learn what each caller watches.
+That is why it is **off unless the operator turns it on**, and why, on, it
+answers only the callers the operator allows. Wallets that use the nodes of
+others find their outputs with compact block filters, which name nothing
+(see [compact-filters.md](compact-filters.md)).
+
+```ini
+rpc.scantxoutset = true            # off by default
+rpc.scantxoutset_caller = 100.64.0.7   # optional; repeat for more callers
+```
+
+**Who it answers.**
+
+- JSON-RPC only, behind the RPC credentials; never a P2P message, and no
+  other method answers it.
+- Off, every call gets `-32601` (method not found) with a message that names
+  `rpc.scantxoutset`. A client that falls back to another source when a node
+  does not have the call (Pickaxe asks an Electrum server) keeps working, and
+  the operator reads which setting turns it on.
+- On, it answers callers on loopback (`127.0.0.0/8`, `::1`, and those as
+  IPv4-mapped IPv6), and each address named by `rpc.scantxoutset_caller`, such
+  as the operator's own computer calling the node over Tailscale. Every other
+  caller gets `-32601` with a message naming `rpc.scantxoutset_caller`, before
+  its parameters are read.
+- Each `rpc.scantxoutset_caller` is one IP address. A host name, a range, the
+  wildcard addresses `0.0.0.0` and `::`, a multicast or the broadcast address
+  is refused at startup.
+- With it on and `rpc.bind` beyond loopback, the node logs one warning at
+  startup that names `rpc.scantxoutset` and the allowed callers.
+
+**What it keeps.** Nothing of a request outlives its answer. The scan objects
+and the outputs found are logged at no level, appear in no error message (an
+error names a scan object by its position, `scanobjects[2]`), and are not
+kept: the request body, its parsed copies, what the walk found and the answer
+are zeroed once the answer is written, and when the scan is aborted, fails or
+its connection closes. Only counts are logged, at debug level.
+
+**Limits.** One scan at a time; a second `start` gets `-8` "Scan already in
+progress, use action "abort" or "status"". At most 1000 scan objects per scan
+(BCHN takes any number, each up to a million scripts for a ranged descriptor
+and a thousand by default). A `raw()` script of at most 10000 bytes, the
+longest locking bytecode a spendable output has. An answer of at most 32 MiB,
+counted as an upper bound while outputs are found; a scan that finds more ends
+with `-8`.
+
+**Actions and answers**, as BCHN 29.1.0 gives them:
+
+- `start` scans for the scan objects and answers `success` (false when the scan
+  was aborted, with what was found until then), `searched_items` (the outputs
+  of the set the walk visited), `unspents` in outpoint order, each with `txid`,
+  `vout`, `scriptPubKey` (hex), `amount`, `height` and, for an output with
+  tokens, `tokenData` as `gettxout` shows it; `total_amount`; and
+  `token_total_amounts`, the fungible amount of each category as a string,
+  when token amounts were found.
+- `status` answers `{"progress": n}`, in %, while a scan runs, and `null`
+  otherwise.
+- `abort` stops the running scan and answers `true`, or `false` when none runs.
+
+**Scan objects** are strings or `{"desc": "...", "range": n}` objects:
+`addr(<address>)` (a cashaddr of the node's network, with or without its
+prefix, token-aware or not, P2PKH, P2SH or P2SH32, or a legacy address),
+`raw(<hex locking bytecode>)` and `tok(<64-hex category>)`. kth takes no
+ranged descriptors and no keys: `pk()`, `pkh()`, `combo()`, `multi()` and
+`sh()` are refused with `-5`, saying that only `addr()`, `raw()` and `tok()`
+are taken. A `range`, which only ranged descriptors use, is checked as BCHN
+checks it and otherwise ignored.
+
+**How the walk shares the set.** The walk holds a read lease on the UTXO set,
+not the exclusive window, so lookups, mempool admission and templates go on
+alongside it. Every 1024 entries it asks whether block connection waits for
+the window; if so it stops there, lets the block in, and starts again from the
+beginning. A block therefore never waits more than 1024 entries for a scan,
+and a scan that answers `success: true` saw the whole set as it stood between
+two blocks. While blocks come faster than one walk takes, as in the initial
+sync, the scan keeps starting over; `status` shows the progress of the current
+walk and `abort` ends it.
+
+Differences from BCHN: the access rules and limits above; error messages that
+name a scan object by its position, not its text; and the answer has the
+fields of BCHN 29.1.0, not the `height` and `bestblock` and the per-output
+`coinbase`, `blockhash` and `confirmations` that BCHN 29.2.0 added. A build
+with the option `utxoz_reference`, whose UTXO set holds places in the block
+files and not outputs, refuses `start` with `-32601`.
+
